// EP-lab procedure (brief B22): typical flutter and CTI ablation; needs Tier 2. The idealised atria
// are closed spheres, so the tricuspid opening and the circuit's outer barrier are drawn as patches.
Scenario(
    format_version: 1,
    id: "ep_flutter",
    title: "EP lab: typical atrial flutter and cavotricuspid-isthmus ablation",
    description: "A macro-re-entrant wave turns around the tricuspid annulus. Entrain it from the isthmus and from outside the circuit, compare the post-pacing intervals, and cut the circuit with a line across the isthmus.",
    tier: ReactionDiffusion,
    anatomy: "default",
    seed: 22,
    notes: Some("Typical flutter is a macro-re-entry around the tricuspid annulus, bounded behind by the crista terminalis and the venae cavae, that must pass through the narrow isthmus between the annulus and the inferior vena cava.\nPacing a little faster than the tachycardia entrains it. If the site is in the circuit the first return beat comes after about one cycle: post-pacing interval minus tachycardia cycle length of 30 ms or less. Outside the circuit the interval is longer by the round trip to the circuit.\nA line of block across the isthmus cuts the only path: the wave dies against the line and sinus rhythm returns.\nThe model is a teaching drawing. The atria of this heart are closed spheres with no holes, so the annulus and an insulating band around the circuit are drawn as inert tissue, and the wave is pre-seeded rather than induced.\n\nSources:\n- Cosio FG et al. Radiofrequency ablation of the inferior vena cava-tricuspid valve isthmus in common atrial flutter. Am J Cardiol 1993;71:705-709.\n- Waldo AL et al. Entrainment and interruption of atrial flutter with atrial pacing: studies in man following open heart surgery. Circulation 1977;56:737-745.\n- Saoudi N et al. A classification of atrial flutter and regular atrial tachycardia according to electrophysiological mechanisms and anatomical bases. Eur Heart J 2001;22:1162-1182."),
    confused_with: ["ep_af_pvi", "atrial tachycardia with 2:1 block"],
    timing: TimingParams(
        sinus: SinusParams(rate_bpm: 70.0),
        av: AvParams(fast: AvPathway(min_ms: 80.0, recovery_amplitude_ms: 200.0, recovery_tau_ms: 100.0, erp_ms: 300.0, blocked: false)),
    ),
    reentry: Some(ReentrySpec(
        tier2: (voxel_mm: 2.0, sense_gap_ms: 150.0),
        patches: [
            Patch(target: Compartment(Atrial), effect: Scale(apd: 0.4, cv: 0.7)),
            Patch(target: Region(CavotricuspidIsthmus), effect: Scale(apd: 1.0, cv: 0.7)),
            // the tricuspid annulus
            Patch(
                target: Band(compartment: Atrial, centre_mm: (-47.9, 27.9, -37.6), radius_mm: 27.0, axis: (0.384, 0.039, 0.923), from_deg: 0.0, to_deg: 28.0),
                effect: Obstacle,
            ),
            // the outer barrier of the circuit, inside the septal contact with the left atrium; one gap lets the wave out to the rest of the atria, a dead end
            Patch(
                target: Band(compartment: Atrial, centre_mm: (-47.9, 27.9, -37.6), radius_mm: 27.0, axis: (0.384, 0.039, 0.923), from_deg: 66.0, to_deg: 80.0, gap_at_mm: Some((-60.3, 14.6, -24.8)), gap_mm: 8.0),
                effect: Obstacle,
            ),
        ],
        initiation: PhaseWave(
            compartment: Atrial,
            start_ms: 600.0,
            centre_mm: Some((-47.9, 27.9, -37.6)),
            axis: (-0.384, -0.039, -0.923),
            span: 2.0,
            within: Some(PhaseWaveLimit(radius_mm: 27.0, polar_deg: (114.0, 152.0))),
        ),
    )),
    ep: Some(EpSpec(goal: TerminateReentry, targets: [CtiLine])),
)
